Paths
description: the colon grammar - one meaning for the colon everywhere
- >
  Path is an expression, that allows to find certain node in Yamlover tree by starting from some node
  and the following the relations in the directed graph

- >
  The colon is THE key marker of the JSON/YAML family: `key: value` means "this key leads to that value", and
  also it is a path separator

- >
    The path consists of segments, separated by colons. In the beginning of the path there can be zero, one, two
    or three colons.
- >
    Path shows how to walk inside the yamlover graph. For example, consider this tree:
- !!yo
    humans:
      - name: Alice
        pets:
          - name: Whiskers
            species: cat
          - name: Bubbles
            species: fish

- !!<*yamlover: $defs: xyflow>
    humans:
      - name: Alice
        pets:
          - name: Whiskers
            species: cat
          - name: Bubbles
            species: fish

- >
    The path to the name `Bubbles` inside this tree will be

- !!<format: text/plain> |
  humans: 0: pets: 1: name

- >
   which would mean

- !!<*:: yamlover: $defs: bullets>
  - enter into the child node keyed `humans`
  - then enter into 0th child node
  - then enter into the child node keyed `pets`
  - then enter the 1st child node
  - then enter into the child node keyed `name`

- Segment can be

- !!<*yamlover: $defs: table>
  - - Known token
    - `~`, `null`, `..`, `...`, `<<`, `-`
    - 'Works according to its meaning: `~` the null key, `..` the parent, `...` descent (queries), `-` the keyless segment - a bookmark''s trailing append, or any position in a query'
  - - Unquoted key
    - `abc`, `cde`
    - Leads to child node, connected by this key
  - - Integer
    - `0`, `1`, `100`
    - Leads to child of this ordinal number
  - - Quoted string
    - `"abc"`, `'cde'`, `"12"`
    - Leads to child node, connected by this key

- The beginning of the path can be

- !!<*yamlover: $defs: table>
  - - (none)
    - ~
    - Starting location is the current node
  - - Single colon
    - `:`
    - Starting location is document boundary
  - - Double colon
    - `::`
    - Starting location is project root
  - - Triple colon
    - `:::`
    - Starting location is world, global Internet or Registry
  - - Double dot
    - `..`
    - Starting location is the parent node
- >
  The retired bracket index `[n]` reads forever as an alias, written never. The slash is an
  ordinary key character: `text/html` rides bare, and `*/pets` is a (dangling) reference to the
  literal key "/pets", never a rooted path. Which bare tokens must be quoted to read as string
  keys - and how metacharacters escape - is the [escaping chapter](::language:pointers:escaping)'s
  subject.
